## Details

### Arguments

| Attribute | | |
|---|---|---|
| `installer` | required | The installer image by digest, `<prefix>/<name>-installer@sha256:<64 hex>` (lower-case hex, no tag beside the digest), as `stowage ship` prints it. An installer carries one release, so the digest is the release. `<name>` is the project's name: `describe` must report the same, or the create is refused. |
| `inputs` | optional map | Non-secret inputs, each given as `--set KEY=VALUE`. They are on the target's command line and in state. A key is a letter or `_`, then letters, digits or `_`; `tls` is refused (the answers file's reserved section). An input given a non-empty value here that `describe` marks `secret` (its `inputs`, and the inputs a TLS mode adds, `tls_inputs`: the secret `STOWAGE_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` of `dns-cloudflare`, from Stowage 0.38.0) is refused: at create, and at an update (every key of the map, changed or not) for the running release and the one an upgrade goes to, before anything runs. A key taken out of the map is given empty (`--set KEY=`) to the re-run: the installer's rule for an empty value decides (a required input is refused, an advanced one goes back to its default); a key taken out that `secret_inputs_wo` now holds is not (`--set` would win over its answer), and, given another value than `inputs` gave, needs a new `secret_inputs_wo_version` in the same apply (else a plan error, `an input moved to secret_inputs_wo`, on every target; OpenTofu plans again with the final configuration before an apply, so an apply never sees one). A refresh reads the value the install records (`status --json --values`) for each key given and keeps it in the resource's private state: one whose record changed since the last record (the refresh before, or the end of the last create or update, which reads it too) and differs from the configuration's (a kit taken gave it, a re-run by hand) is put in state, so the next plan gives the configuration's again; a value the installer records otherwise than given (an empty one as its default) records the same each time, so it is no change; a file input is compared by its digest. |
| `secret_inputs_wo` | optional map, **write-only** | Secret inputs, never in plan or state, sent on standard input as an answers file (`--answers`). A key in both maps is refused. |
| `secret_inputs_wo_version` | optional number | Change it to apply a change of `secret_inputs_wo` (a write-only value is never compared): the update re-sends every secret input the running release declares, a TLS mode's (`STOWAGE_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`) included; without a change an upgrade sends only those the new release adds. Leaving it out with `secret_inputs_wo` set is a warning. |
| `secret_rotations` | optional map of numbers | A number per generated secret of the release (`{ SESSION_SECRET = 1 }`), as `random_password`'s `keepers`: a changed number rotates that secret in that apply, by the installer the install keeps — `<short>-stow secrets rotate KEY --yes --no-input --dir ./<name>` for a secret whose spec has `replace: true` (one pass: nothing of the old value kept), or its three passes `--stage`, the swap (`secrets rotate KEY`) and `--finish` for one with `rotation: true`, each bringing the stack up with it. A key's first number — at the create, or a key added later — is recorded and rotates nothing; a key taken out is forgotten. A key the release does not rotate on this target (`describe --json` `secrets[].rotate`: `none`, or no way named for the target), or that is no generated secret of it, is refused (`KEY does not rotate on SHAPE`, `not a generated secret of the release`; a way this provider does not run: `KEY rotates in a way this provider does not run`): at the create, before the install; at plan, when the plan keeps `installer` and the provider read the `describe` of the release state runs (at the create, a rotation, or, while `secret_rotations` is set, an update after which the ways kept are not the running release's — a read that fails then is a warning, `… (the release's rotate ways not read)` —, kept in the resource's private state; not after a refresh that reads another release, nor for a state made by an earlier provider or an import never applied with `secret_rotations`); else by the apply, after the update's other steps (saved) and before any rotation. A release whose `describe` lists no `secrets` (one built by Stowage before 0.46.0) takes none (`secret_rotations needs a newer release`). The keys rotate in key order, after the update's other steps, on the release the install runs then, within the update's timeout; the first that fails stops the rest, which keep their number (the next plan shows them again), and a rotation by passes resumes in the next apply at the pass that failed (`The rotation of KEY stopped at …`; running a pass again is safe: one whose change the installer recorded brings the stack up with it), the passes before not run again. A resumed pass that fails again keeps the resume point (`Rotating KEY stopped part-way`); when the target's rotation was finished or moved on by hand since, take the key out, apply, then give it back: the apply that adds it records its number and forgets where the rotation stopped, so its next rotation starts at `--stage`. A first pass refused changed nothing and leaves no resume point, and a key whose way is no longer `passes` (another release) drops its own; a stopped rotation whose number is given back to state's is a plan warning (`the rotation of KEY is not finished`). |
| `given_wo` | optional, **write-only** | What another install gives (the installer's `--given` YAML), used at create, on standard input. |
| `secrets_wo` | optional, **write-only** | A decrypted `secrets export` of a machine made again (`--secrets`), used at create, on standard input. |
| `tls` | optional | The front's TLS mode, `--tls`: `acme`, `dns-cloudflare`, `byo` or `internal`. Left out, loopback mode. A change is given to the re-run (`--tls`); taking it out is a plan error. |
| `domains` | optional map | Route → domain, `--domains route=host,…` (sorted). A route name is a letter or digit, then letters, digits, `_ . -`; a value holding `,`, `=` or a space is refused. A changed or added route is given to the re-run; taking one out, or adding one while `installer` changes, is a plan error. |
| `ports` | optional map of numbers | Route → port (1–65535; checked again at apply, for a port computed from another resource), `--ports route=port,…`. A changed port is given to the re-run; when the release changes, a route the new release adds (its `describe` `routes` lists it and the running release's does not) goes to the upgrade's `--ports`, every other to the re-run; taking one out is a plan error. |
| `address` | optional | The loopback address the routes bind, `--address`. First-install only: a read records the install's (`status --json` `address`) when state names another, or when it was left out and the install binds another than `127.0.0.1`, so the next plan shows the change, a plan error. |
| `acme_ca` | optional | Another ACME directory than Let's Encrypt's, its URL (`--acme-ca URL`), in the `acme` and `dns-cloudflare` modes (the installer refuses it in another). At create it goes to the first install; a change to the re-run of the running release; taken out, `--acme-ca none` (Let's Encrypt's again). A refresh in those modes reads the one the install holds (`status --json --values` `acme_ca`; none: null), so a change made outside OpenTofu shows in the next plan. |
| `acme_ca_root` | optional | PEM of the root the ACME directory's certificates chain to (`--acme-ca-root`, a file on standard input), e.g. `file("acme-root.pem")`; not PEM is refused at validation (`acme_ca_root is not PEM`). At create it goes to the first install; a change to the re-run; taken out, `--acme-ca-root none`. A refresh in the ACME modes compares its `sha256` with the one the install holds (`status --json --values` `acme_ca_root`): none held puts null in state, another root its digest (`sha256:<hex>`), so the next plan gives the configuration's root again; a root that cannot be read (`acme_ca_root_error`) changes nothing. |
| `registry_login_wo` | optional, **write-only** | The install's own registry login: a Docker config holding one `auths` entry for the host of the release's registry or of the provider's `registry` (`{"auths": {"HOST": {"auth": "BASE64(USER:PASSWORD)"}}}`), minted for this install in the registry (revoking it leaves every other install pulling). Never on a command line or in state: a file on standard input, in a private directory removed afterwards. Sent at create and when `registry_login_wo_version` changes (a version given without it is refused at validation, `registry_login_wo_version without registry_login_wo`; an empty one is an apply error, `registry_login_wo is not given`); left out with its version, the install keeps the login it holds. The installer refuses a login for another host, or one that does not read, before anything is written. The first install's `--registry-login-file`; a re-run's when the version changes. |
| `registry_login_wo_version` | optional number | Change it to send `registry_login_wo` again. `registry_login_wo` without it is a warning; it without `registry_login_wo` (null) is refused at validation (`registry_login_wo_version without registry_login_wo`). A refresh that reads the install holding no login (`status --json --values` `pull_login` false, `pull_login_error` empty) takes it out of state, so the next plan sends the login again. |
| `kit_wo` | optional, **write-only** | A handoff kit, never in plan or state: the ephemeral `stowage_kit`'s `kit`, issued in the same apply (see [handoff kits](../guides/handoff-kits.md)), or a kit made by hand (`file("x.kit")`), given to the first install (`--kit`, a file on standard input) with `kit_code_wo` (`--kit-code-file`, on standard input). Without its code at create, an apply error before anything runs (`kit_code_wo is not given`). First-install only: a create that finds an install there (Docker's `status --json` answers only once its `.env` is written) runs its re-run, which gives neither, as the other first-install settings; a kit for an install that exists is `stowage_kit_take`'s. With `given_wo`, refused at validation (`kit_wo with given_wo`). |
| `kit_code_wo` | optional, **write-only** | The kit's code (the ephemeral `stowage_kit`'s `code`), never on a command line or in state, sent at create alone; without `kit_wo` or `kit_wo_version`, refused at validation (`kit_code_wo without kit_wo`). It may be left out once the install took the kit. |
| `kit_wo_version` | optional string | Says a kit is given at create: `stowage_kit`'s `id`, or any value for a kit made by hand. With it, a create whose `kit_wo` is null (the ephemeral `stowage_kit` is null outside the apply that issued the kit, so an apply that failed after issuing it lost it) is an apply error before the install runs (`The kit is not here`, naming the fix: bump the `stowage_kit`'s `version`, which issues a kit again in the same apply), never an install without the kit; a create finding the install made (a retry after a create that failed once the install was done) gives no kit and needs none, the take-over's warning naming `kit_wo`. An install there, its first install finished, failed or marked as running (`status --json` `installing`; a run cut by a timeout leaves the mark), is run again the same way, needing no kit: one still running is refused by the installer, and an apply once it ends goes on. Another project's install there, or one made otherwise, is refused first, before any kit is asked for. A later change runs nothing on the target, with a plan warning (`kit_wo is first-install only`, said too when it is not known yet because its `stowage_kit` issues a kit in this apply: the install takes nothing unless the plan replaces it), and is recorded. With `given_wo`, refused at validation (`kit_wo with given_wo`). |
| `allow_rollback` | default `false` | Lets a change of `installer` to `previous.installer` run the kept installer's `rollback`. Without it, that change is a plan error (and an apply error when `status --json` names it `previous` only at apply); while it is not known yet (a value from another resource), the apply decides, `a rollback goes alone` included. |
| `deletion_protection` | default `true` | While true, a destroy is refused (`deletion_protection is on`). Set it `false` and apply first. |
| `delete_data` | default `false` | A destroy runs `uninstall --data` (the volumes deleted) instead of `uninstall` (kept). |
| `plan_dry_run` | default `false` | On Docker, a plan changing `installer` alone (no re-run of the running release beside it) runs the change's dry run on the target, the step chosen from the install's `status --json` by the apply's own rule (its errors — `a rollback needs allow_rollback`, `installer is another digest of the release installed`, first-install settings the install holds otherwise — at plan, or the apply's alone), so the installer's refusal is a plan error (its reason the summary, its fix the detail): an upgrade's — `sh -c '…' sh <work_dir>` removes the plans' directories older than an hour there, then makes a private (0700) `<work_dir>/.stowage-plan.XXXXXX` beside the install; the new installer is fetched into it (as at create, logged in the same way) and described, the inputs checked as the apply checks them (a secret input in `inputs` refused before anything runs), then `sh ./<name>.sh upgrade --dry-run --no-input --json --dir <work_dir>/<name> [--set K=V]… [--ports]` runs in that directory (what it extracts stays there) with what the upgrade would be given (the inputs, secret inputs as `--answers` and ports the new release adds), and the directory is removed (only a `.stowage-plan.XXXXXX` of the work directory is; a removal that fails is a warning); a rollback's — the inputs checked as for the upgrade (a secret input in `inputs`, and on this machine an input the run's environment sets, refused before anything runs), then the kept installer's `rollback --dry-run --json`, without the inputs' variables as the apply's. A plan that also re-runs the running release gets a warning instead (`No dry run of this plan`: the upgrade runs on what that re-run leaves; told before anything is fetched when the change is the running release's whatever the new one is: TLS, domains, a secret input, an input it declares that the plan gives, a port of a route it has), a rollback with one is `a rollback goes alone` (as at apply, its status's `previous` deciding a rollback); an install gone since its status was read gets a warning too (the apply says what it finds); an input moved to `secret_inputs_wo` without a new version is refused before anything is fetched; one whose configuration holds a value not known yet (but `deletion_protection`, `delete_data` and `timeouts`, which no dry run reads), or with a setting a run reads not known yet (the pull account, `registry`, `registry_ca`, the mirror's login, `public_keys`), no dry run, with a warning (`No dry run of this plan yet`: OpenTofu plans again at apply, with it known, and that plan runs it); an install whose home `work_dir` was never resolved (an import's), nothing but a warning (`Taking over the install: its values are not read at plan`).  A failure is a plan error too. The fetched image stays in the daemon's store, and the dry run takes the install's lock while it reads it (an apply of the same install meanwhile is refused by it). Opt-in: the plan reaches the target, and OpenTofu plans again at apply, which runs it again. On an imported state never applied it runs too, and says what the take-over changes (`Taking over the install: …`); its rollback is refused (`a rollback of an imported install`). On Kubernetes and the VM: see their guides (the new installer's `k8s upgrade --dry-run` or `k8s rollback --dry-run`; the VM's `upgrade --version V --dry-run` or `rollback --dry-run` over SSH, Stowage 0.38.0 or later on its OS). |
| `timeouts` | block | `create` (default 30m; it bounds the wait for a first install still `installing`), `read` (5m), `update` (30m), `delete` (15m). A run cut by its timeout is a failure. |

A null value in `inputs`, `secret_inputs_wo`, `domains` or `ports` is refused by its key
(`<attribute>.<key> is null`); a value not known until apply (an ephemeral variable's, one computed
from another resource) is not, its key checked when the configuration is validated.

### The connection

| Attribute | | |
|---|---|---|
| `host` | optional | The Docker host, reached by the system `ssh`. Left out, the install is on the machine running OpenTofu, and `user`, `port`, `private_key_file` and `known_hosts_line` are refused. |
| `port` | optional | SSH port (1–65535); left out, 22. |
| `user` | required with `host` | SSH user. A host or user starting with `-` or holding a space is refused. |
| `private_key_file` | optional | The private key's path; left out, ssh's agent and default keys. |
| `known_hosts_line` | required with `host` | The host's `known_hosts` line, `host keytype base64` (the host field may be left out, hashed or a pattern: it is replaced). That key alone is accepted. A marker (`@cert-authority`, `@revoked`), an unknown key type, a key that is not base64 or more than one line is refused. |
| `work_dir` | optional | The directory on the target holding `<name>.sh` and the install directory `<name>/`: an absolute, clean path (no `.` or `..` element, no `//`, no trailing `/`; else refused). Left out, the SSH user's home (locally, OpenTofu's working directory), which the create resolves first into `id`: `$HOME` when it is the directory the run starts in (their physical paths compared), so a home reached through a symbolic link keeps the link's name; else `cd` there and `pwd -L`. First-install only: a plan naming another directory is an error, but naming the home a create resolved (state's `id`) when it was left out changes nothing; taking out a `work_dir` that was given is a change (that home was never resolved). |

### Attributes read from the target

| Attribute | |
|---|---|
| `id` | `<work_dir>/<name>` (the home resolved when `work_dir` is left out). |
| `short_name` | The installer's short name: a Docker install keeps its installer as `<id>/<short_name>-stow` (`describe`'s at create, then `status --json` `short_name` at each read and update, so a rollback across a release that renamed it finds the one kept); a VM runs its OS bundle's `<short_name>-stow`. |
| `registry_ca_digest` | `sha256:<hex>` of the registry CA the install keeps (`status --json` `registry_ca`; on Kubernetes, the provider's at the install), null for none. On Docker and the VM a plan whose provider `registry_ca` has another digest (none included) is an error (`the provider's registry_ca is first-install only`), but for an imported state never applied. An imported state holds none until its first apply, which records it (planned as known after apply); on a VM whose status reports none, the provider's. |
| `version`, `revision` | The release installed (`status --json` `installed`). |
| `channel` | The channel the install follows. |
| `status` | `installed`, `installing` (the first install has not finished; helm's `pending-install` on Kubernetes), `failed: <why>` (`install_failed` on Docker; a latest helm revision neither deployed nor pending on Kubernetes), `pending: <why>` (Kubernetes: helm working on the release), or `not installed`. |
| `previous` | `{version, revision, installer}`, the release `rollback` returns to; null before an upgrade. |
| `registry` | The provider's `registry` at the install; null in an imported state until its first apply. |

### Plan-time errors

A change of a first-install-only setting — `work_dir`, `address`, the provider's `registry` and
`registry_ca`, or the project an `installer` names (an imported state: its install directory's
name) — is a plan error naming the old value, never a replacement: replacing an install deletes
its data.

A key of `secret_rotations` added or changed that the release does not rotate on this target is a plan error when the provider read the release's ways before and the plan keeps the release (see `secret_rotations`).

Also plan errors: `installer` changed to `previous.installer` without `allow_rollback` (`a
rollback needs allow_rollback`); `tls` taken out (`tls cannot be removed`: a re-run has no flag
for loopback mode); a route taken out of `domains` or `ports` (`domains.<route> cannot be
removed`); a rollback with any other change of what the target applies (`a rollback goes
alone`); a route added to `domains` while `installer` changes (known at plan time) (`a domain
added with a release change`: a route the new release adds takes its domain from its domain
input, in `inputs`; one the running release has takes it in an apply of its own). A plan
changing anything the target applies plans `status`, `version`, `revision`, `channel` and
`previous` unknown (the update reads them after its run, and the target may run another release
than state says: changed outside OpenTofu since the last refresh); one changing `installer` also
`short_name`. An imported state's first apply also plans `registry` and `registry_ca_digest`
unknown (it records them).

